  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@type": "Article",
  "headline": "DSA Compliance Made Simple for Comment Moderation",
  "description": "How to meet EU Digital Services Act requirements for comment moderation: written reasons, full audit trail, transparency reporting and EU hosting.",
  "datePublished": "2026-06-08",
  "author": { "@type": "Organization", "name": "OpenModeration", "url": "https://www.openmoderation.com" },
  "publisher": {
    "@type": "Organization",
    "name": "OpenModeration",
    "logo": { "@type": "ImageObject", "url": "https://www.openmoderation.com/logo.svg" }
  },
  "image": "https://www.openmoderation.com/og-image.jpg",
  "mainEntityOfPage": { "@type": "WebPage", "@id": "https://www.openmoderation.com/blog/dsa-compliance-open-source-moderation" },
  "dateModified": "2026-06-08"
};
---

<BaseLayout title="DSA Compliance Made Simple for Comment Moderation" description="How to meet EU Digital Services Act requirements for comment moderation: written reasons, full audit trail, transparency reporting and EU hosting." jsonLd={articleJsonLd}>
<Breadcrumb items={[{ label: "Home", href: "/" }, { label: "Blog", href: "/blog" }, { label: "DSA Compliance Made Simple" }]} />
  <article class="mx-auto max-w-3xl px-4 py-16 sm:px-6 lg:px-8">
    <div class="mb-8">
      <div class="flex items-center gap-3 text-sm text-surface-400">
        <time datetime="2026-06-08">June 8, 2026</time>
        <span>&middot;</span>
        <span>6 min read</span>
      </div>
      <h1 class="mt-4 text-3xl font-bold tracking-tight text-surface-900 sm:text-4xl">DSA Compliance Made Simple for Comment Moderation</h1>
    </div>

    <div class="prose prose-surface max-w-none text-surface-600">
      <p class="text-lg leading-relaxed">
        The EU Digital Services Act (DSA) came into full effect in 2024, and platforms are
        still adjusting to it. Any service that hosts user content for people in the EU, wherever it is
        headquartered, must act on notices of illegal content, explain its moderation decisions
        and let users contest them. Fines can reach 6% of global annual turnover.
      </p>
      <p>
        For many platforms, the hard part is proving to regulators that their moderation
        system works. That takes three things: an automated moderation
        pipeline, a complete audit trail, and the ability to generate transparency reports.
      </p>

      <h2 class="text-xl font-bold text-surface-900">The DSA requirements, simplified</h2>
      <p>The DSA doesn't prescribe a specific technology. It requires outcomes:</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Notice and action (Art. 16):</strong> Users must be able to flag content. Platforms must act on notices.</li>
        <li><strong>Transparency reporting (Art. 15):</strong> Platforms must publish reports on content moderation activities.</li>
        <li><strong>Statement of reasons (Art. 17):</strong> Every removal or restriction must be explained to the user, including whether automated means were used.</li>
        <li><strong>Complaint system (Art. 20):</strong> Users must be able to appeal moderation decisions.</li>
        <li><strong>Risk assessment (Art. 34):</strong> Very large platforms (over 45 million monthly users in the EU) must assess systemic risks.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2 class="text-xl font-bold text-surface-900">Why explained decisions matter for compliance</h2>
      <p>
        DSA compliance officers should ask a simple question. If your moderation tool is
        a black box, how will you show a regulator what it does?
      </p>
      <p>
        A tool that only returns "removed" or "kept" leaves you trusting its claims, and that
        trust might not satisfy a regulator. You need to see why each decision was made.
      </p>
      <p>
        With OpenModeration, every decision comes with a reason written in plain language and
        is recorded in a full audit trail. Your compliance team can see exactly which rule was
        applied, what criteria were used, and where the results go.
      </p>

      <h2 class="text-xl font-bold text-surface-900">Building a DSA-compliant moderation pipeline</h2>
      <p>Here's what a DSA-compliant setup looks like with OpenModeration:</p>
      <ol>
        <li><strong>Content ingestion:</strong> Receive content via API, webhook, or social connectors. Every item gets a unique ID and is stored encrypted.</li>
        <li><strong>AI moderation:</strong> The request goes to the best provider. Results include scores per category and a flagged/not-flagged decision.</li>
        <li><strong>Rule evaluation:</strong> Your rules combine forbidden words for instant blocking, category thresholds for automated decisions, and your own rules written in plain language.</li>
        <li><strong>Audit logging:</strong> Every step is logged: content, provider, scores, rule matched, action taken, timestamp.</li>
        <li><strong>Transparency export:</strong> GET /v1/analytics/usage provides the data needed for DSA transparency reports.</li>
      </ol>

      <h2 class="text-xl font-bold text-surface-900">Meeting specific DSA requirements</h2>
      <p>
        <strong>Audit trail:</strong> OpenModeration logs every moderation with provider, latency, scores, and decision.
        Data is retained per your configured policy (default 7 days for input text, 90 days for logs).
        All exportable for regulatory audits.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Transparency reporting:</strong> The analytics API provides usage data by period, provider, and category.
        It covers total requests, flagged rates and provider performance, which is what DSA transparency reports ask for.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Data sovereignty:</strong> Data is hosted in the EU and you can export it at any time.
        This matters for GDPR compliance and for DSA risk assessments. On-premise deployment is
        possible on request: <a href="https://calendly.com/pierre-logora/60min" class="text-brand-600 hover:text-brand-500">let's talk</a>.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Human review:</strong> The manual review queue allows human moderators to handle edge cases,
        meeting DSA requirements for complaint handling and appeals.
      </p>
    </div>
  </article>

